== in-world-instruments-and-inspect-view
{
 "gdd": "in-world-instruments-and-inspect-view",
 "blocking": [
  "Detailed Rules IW-11(e), IW-12, IW-13 dispatch table (L272-274), S2 FILTER_DRIVEN row (L518); Interactions I-A; Formulas D.5 Output Range; E-IW-33, E-IW-35; AC-IW-19, -20, -44, -69 (vs perception-filter.md PF-8 L269-270, PF-17 L349, motion-state L554; registry L1411): [main review, confirmed by creative-director] The Instruments GDD never mentions the Filter's tenth Perceived field tremor_weight_pm or the BREATH motion_mode (grep finds 0 hits). The Filter is the publisher and the registry source. PF-8 L270 and PF-17 L349 say an honest tremor that is running at lie onset is blended out over TREMOR_BLEND_S := WANDER_ONSET_S, not stopped. PF L335/L554 send BREATH for static-pair frames, and registry L1411 records the amendment. IW S2 L518 says instead 'triple, tremor and notch cleared that frame', and the IW-13 dispatch table has no BREATH row. AC-IW-20/69 contradict AC-PF-46/85 at the same interface, so both test suites cannot pass. G1 says the publisher wins. This also matters for feel: a tremor that stops dead at lie onset is itself a tell, which is the opposite of what the Filter's onset rule is for. FIX: Add tremor_weight_pm to I-A and IW-5. Define the drawn tremor as round(TREMOR_GAIN x A_eff x env x s x tremor_weight_pm/1000), clamped as in D.5. Reword IW-11(e), IW-12, S2 and the D.5 Output Range to say: no NEW tremor starts in a non-HONEST frame; a tremor already running fades by tremor_weight_pm; the offset is 0 once the weight is 0. Keep the clearing of the reduced-motion notch and of the settle triple immediate, and say so explicitly. Add a BREATH row to IW-13 and S2: frame_override held still, the gleam breathes via leak_amplitude_pm, no needle motion, no settle, no tremor. Rewrite AC-IW-20, -44, -69 and E-IW-33/35 to match, and add AC-IW-19 legs for BREATH and for the tremor fade."
 ]
}
== pixel-perfect-render-pipeline-camera
{
 "gdd": "pixel-perfect-render-pipeline-camera",
 "blocking": [
  "Acceptance Criteria > Camera follow, look-ahead, cuts (mouse-aim AC, L1059); Formulas D.14b Output Range/Example (L644-645); Edge Cases L801; Player Controller AC-PCI-056: [main review, confirmed by creative-director] L1059 says the settled-run aim shift is 'exactly f_q - p, 32 px west ... 32 = floor(32.4)' and calls the aim 'constant'. The same GDD gives the after-update steady lag as lag_ss = 30.9 px at 60 fps and 31.8 px at 144 fps (L161, L438-452, L1050, L1069), so the oracle is wrong at every shipped rate. f_q is floored from the interpolated target each frame, while AIM_ORIGIN steps once per tick, so 'constant' does not hold at 90 or 144 fps either. The D.14b Example contradicts itself: floor(1000 - 32.4) = 967 is a 33 px shift, labelled as 32 px trailing. A correct implementation fails this (auto) AC, which is the G4 defect class of the three earlier blockers. The sibling has not fixed it: Player Controller still quotes 32.4 as the lag (L132, L159, L491). FIX: Recompute from D.6/D.14b (G4). Give the fixture dt explicitly and use lag_ss(dt), as L1069 already does. Change the oracle to 'aim-point shift within 1 base px of -(lag_ss(dt) + tick phase), exactly constant only at 60 fps with integer targets, varying by no more than ceil(v/f_render) + 1 base px at 90 and 144 fps'. Correct the D.14b Example arithmetic. State in D.14b and Edge Cases L801 that 32.4 px is the dt -> 0 upper bound. Have Player Controller mirror the bound in AC-PCI-056 and its Edge Case."
 ]
}
== player-controller-input
{
 "gdd": "player-controller-input",
 "blocking": [
  "PC-1(b), PC-4 rule 2, D.8, PC-17; interaction-system.md IA-36 (L303) and Dependencies L893; modular-ship-assembly.md L155, L491, L1046, AC-MSA-83 (L1641); OQ 30(b): [main review; creative-director verified] The Controller publishes set_channel_ref(actor, owner_system, channel_id, face_octant). PC-7 L179, PC-17 L220 and D.8 L534/L548 depend on face_octant to set channel_face. Interaction IA-36 L303 still reads 'Controller GDD publishes no mutator for channel_ref' and gives 'proposed names set_channel_ref(actor, owner_system, channel_id)', and L893 marks the four mutator names 'provisional'. Assembly L155 and L491 call the 3-argument form. Neither sibling mentions face_octant or channel_face, so the weld, cut and patch facing lock (a Pillar 1 readability anchor) cannot be built from the siblings' text, and OQ 30(b)'s 'done' claim is false. ACTOR_SELF also has no defined face_octant because there is no target anchor, and D.8 rule 2 has no behaviour for channel_face = none. FIX: Under G6/G1 the Controller is the publisher and the siblings conform. In IA-36, IA-14's begin_* apply and Interaction Dependencies L893, and in Assembly L155, L491, L1046 and AC-MSA-83, use the 4-argument signature with face_octant = octant_of(target anchor - foot) computed by the channel owner at apply. Remove the 'proposed names' and 'provisional' wording. Define face_octant = none for ACTOR_SELF and add to D.8 rule 2 that channel_face = none holds facing_prev. Register the four Class B mutators in entities.yaml. Correct OQ 30(b). Then grep for 'set_channel_ref(actor, ' and confirm zero 3-argument hits (G3).",
  "PC-6 focus model (L177), D.9, PC-2 no-focus ignore (L815), AC-PCI-115; interaction-system.md IA-17 (L231), AC-IA-33b; HUD prompt rule: [main review; creative-director verified] PC-6 focuses only interactables that are in reach, in the arc and lit, and the Adapter submits only for a focused object. A press with no focus is ignored and plays cue_input_ignored. IA-17 declares the ACTOR_SELF row (welder, CHANNEL, payload = own PlayerId) and the act_gate exemption, but no GDD defines how ACTOR_SELF becomes the focus or gets a prompt. The only MVP suit repair, and the lamp-off case that the G1/G6 rulings exist to protect, therefore cannot be started from the controller. AC-PCI-115 and AC-IA-33b inject the command directly, so they pass without proving the input path. FIX: The Controller owns focus and the interact button, so it decides. Add a self-target rule to PC-6 and D.9: while the equipped tool has a declared ACTOR_SELF row and no world candidate is focusable, ACTOR_SELF is the focus with a fixed fallback score above the maximum world score (3.0), so any world candidate wins. Do not gate it on suit integrity: the Controller must not read integrity_bp (an oracle risk), and IA-17's NOTHING_TO_REPAIR guard already handles a healthy suit. The HUD draws the self prompt at the chest keep-out position and does not apply the lit rule. Interaction cites the rule from IA-17. Add AC-PCI: 'welder equipped, no world candidate in reach, lamp off, press interact -> begin_patch {ACTOR_SELF} is submitted and Applied'."
 ]
}
